# Contributing

## Before you start

Open an issue before doing anything larger than a fix, so the approach can be agreed before the
work is done.

## Commit messages

Commit messages are enforced in CI and follow
[Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/): `type(scope): subject`.

Types in use: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`,
`chore`, `revert`. A breaking change is marked with `!` after the type.

The commit message is not decoration: the changelog is generated from it.

## Pull requests

Every pull request answers three questions, because they are the three things a reviewer cannot
get from the diff:

- **Why** — the problem this solves. Link the issue.
- **Scope** — what is included, and what is deliberately not.
- **Evidence** — how a reviewer confirms it works.

## Review vocabulary

Reviews use [Conventional Comments](https://conventionalcomments.org/). A comment prefixed
`issue:` is blocking; `nitpick:`, `suggestion:` and `praise:` are not. This keeps severity
machine-visible instead of buried in tone.

## Code ownership
{% if codeowners_team %}
`.github/CODEOWNERS` names `{{ codeowners_team }}` as the owner of everything in this
repository. GitHub rejects a CODEOWNERS file that names an owner which does not exist, or that
it cannot resolve:

```text
Unknown owner on line 2: make sure the team @<org>/<team> exists, is publicly visible,
and has write access to the repository
```

So that owner must exist **before** this file means anything, it must be visible to the
repository, and it must have write access. Until then, GitHub reports the repository as having
a broken CODEOWNERS file, and a branch protection rule requiring code owner review can block
every pull request.

An owner must be a user with write access (`@username`) or a visible team with write access
(`@org/team-name`); an organisation handle on its own is not a valid owner. If that owner stops
existing, delete the file: an owner that does not exist is worse than no owner, because it
makes the file invalid rather than merely advisory.
{% else %}
This repository has no `.github/CODEOWNERS`, because no owner can be guessed. A valid owner is
a visible team with write access (`@org/team-name`) or a user with write access (`@username`).
An organisation handle on its own is not an owner at all, and a personal account has no teams,
so no default would be right for both.

Name an owner when you have one — re-run the template with `codeowners_team` set, or write the
file yourself:

```text
# .github/CODEOWNERS
* @acme/maintainers
```

An owner that does not exist is worse than no owner: GitHub reports the repository as having a
broken CODEOWNERS file, and a branch protection rule requiring code owner review then blocks
every pull request.
{% endif %}

## Generated files

Files generated from the repository template are never hand-edited: the next template sync
would conflict with the edit. Keep customisations in files no module owns. A failed sync is
the intended outcome when a repository has diverged.

The sync never deletes files either. Turning a module off, or clearing an answer that used to
generate a file, stops that file being rendered and leaves the existing one in place — remove
it yourself, in the same commit.

## Decisions

Significant decisions are recorded as ADRs in `docs/adr/`, including what each one defers and
the trigger that would revisit it.
